This is a classic exercise. I need to define the interfaces ReadCounter, WriteCounter, ReadWriteCounter, and the types readCounter, writeCounter, readWriteCounter.

Important considerations:
- Consistency test: `n, nops := rc.ReadCount()` must be consistent — nops must equal n/numBytes. Since each Read returns len(p) bytes, n is always a multiple of numBytes. To guarantee consistency, ReadCount must atomically read both counters under a single lock.
- testReadTotal: 8000 goroutines each call Read once, each returning 50 bytes. Expected values: n == totalBytes and nops == numGo. That is, each Read operation must increment the operation count by exactly 1, and the byte count by the number of bytes actually read (n).

Design:

```go
package paasio

import (
	"io"
	"sync"
)

type ReadCounter interface {
	io.Reader
	ReadCount() (int64, int)
}

type WriteCounter interface {
	io.Writer
	WriteCount() (int64, int)
}

type ReadWriteCounter interface {
	ReadCounter
	WriteCounter
}

type readCounter struct {
	r     io.Reader
	mu    sync.Mutex
	bytes int64
	ops   int
}

func NewReadCounter(r io.Reader) ReadCounter {
	return &readCounter{r: r}
}

func (rc *readCounter) Read(p []byte) (int, error) {
	n, err := rc.r.Read(p)
	rc.mu.Lock()
	rc.bytes += int64(n)
	rc.ops++
	rc.mu.Unlock()
	return n, err
}

func (rc *readCounter) ReadCount() (int64, int) {
	rc.mu.Lock()
	defer rc.mu.Unlock()
	return rc.bytes, rc.ops
}
```

Wait — testReadTotal: each goroutine calls rc.Read(p) once, nopReader returns len(p)=50. So nops == 8000 requires that each Read increments ops by exactly 1, even when an error occurs. nopReader has a nil error, so it always returns 50 bytes. No problem.

But note: should ops be incremented even when an error occurs? In testReadTotal, since error is nil, it doesn't matter. But for consistency, incrementing ops on every Read call is the safest interpretation.

